librewolf-void
==============
Void does not accept browser forks in void-packages, so this repository
provides prebuilt packages for librewolf.

Installing
----------
Download the release package for x86_64-musl:

librewolf-<version>.xbps

Then install it with:
# xbps-install ./librewolf-<version>.xbps

The package is intentionally built only for x86_64-musl and is tuned for
Intel Skylake-class CPUs, including the i3-6100U. It is not intended for
older or non-Skylake x86-64 CPUs.

Building
--------
Clone void-packages and bootstrap for x86_64-musl:
$ git clone https://github.com/void-linux/void-packages
$ cd void-packages
$ ./xbps-src -A x86_64-musl binary-bootstrap

Clone this repository and copy the librewolf-void/srcpkgs/librewolf folder
into void-packages/srcpkgs, then run:
$ ./xbps-src pkg -j4 -A x86_64-musl librewolf

Lastly, install with:
# xbps-install -R hostdir/binpkgs librewolf

Build target and optimization
----------------------------
This repository intentionally builds only x86_64-musl. The package is tuned
for Intel Skylake-class CPUs, including the i3-6100U, with Clang, -O3,
-march=skylake, -mtune=skylake, LTO, PGO, and single-codegen-unit Rust
optimization.

Integrity
---------
The source archive is pinned by SHA256 in the package template. Release
packages are also accompanied by a .sig2 signature.

For a downloaded package:
$ sha256sum librewolf-<version>.xbps

For the strongest verification, compare the result with the checksum
published by the release workflow.
